Main findings
- Correctness remains stable after lazy env materialization.
- Deopt remains near-zero on main canaries (except `CALL_DEPTH_CAP` on `gab_deriv`).
- Heap/Itrs remains high (~2.3 to ~4.1) and mostly unchanged; this implies lambda env-cell eagerness was not the dominant allocator on admission kernels.
- Remaining cost is likely dominated by:
  - APP-frame construction (`heap_alloc(2)` for pushed APP args),
  - constructor materialization paths,
  - DUP cell traffic in strict recursive code.

Operational lessons
- `--as-c` artifacts still are not parallel-safe; keep telemetry serialized.
- Bench harness output may repeat tables while progressing and exits non-zero on timeout rows.

Next likely steps
1. Add allocator-class telemetry (at least APP-frame cells vs env/DUP cells) on compiled path to identify true heap hotspots.
2. Prototype direct compiled call lowering for static REF APP spines to avoid materializing APP nodes for argument passing.
3. Investigate linear DUP/binder cases to avoid heap cells when DP terms are provably absent in subtree.
4. Address `CALL_DEPTH_CAP` via tail-call lowering first, then trampoline for non-tail recursion/mutual recursion.
